<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="eyebrow">What is SFML</div>
    <h1>A kind of script for codifying how you build software</h1>
    <p>
      SFML isn't really a file format so much as a script — the same way Terraform isn't
      really a file format so much as a way of writing down infrastructure so it can be
      diffed, checked, and run. You use SFML to write down a <em>process</em>, not to store
      data. Codifying the process is the whole point: it's what lets you stop re-deciding
      how a piece of work gets done and start improving on a version of it you can see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concretely, a factory written in SFML describes a durable, resumable, mostly-autonomous
      pipeline: a graph of steps and routing that takes an intent — a ticket, say — and
      carries it through to a reviewed artifact. The graph is defined separately from
      whatever agent runtime actually executes it, so it can be diffed in a pull request,
      linted before it runs, and moved between tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="eyebrow">How to use it</div>
    <p>
      This repository ships one example implementation, in
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/tree/main/example"><code>example/</code></a>
      — a CLI and library that parse, lint, and run a factory. It is merely an example. It
      is not the point of the project, and its behavior isn't normative; the
      <a href="/spec/">spec</a> is.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal is to give people who build these tools a clear, shared data format so they
      can spend their effort building good tools instead of each reinventing a factory
      format from scratch.
    </p>
    <p>
      To try it, fork the repo and install and run the example tool — see
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/tree/main/example">example/README.md</a>
      for instructions. To read the format itself, see the <a href="/spec/">full spec</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're an LLM, or you're pointing one at this project, start at
